{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"peaceable-chunky-harvestmen","creation_channel":"mcp","description":"## Problem\n\n`make verify` is the stated pre-push contract in `AGENTS.md`: it is the one local command that lets an author say \"I verified this\" and be right, and it is the locally checkable half of the `Verified` CI vote. On an agent workstation running under the OS-level filesystem/IPC sandbox, it **cannot produce a verdict at all**.\n\nRoughly 52 errors are raised, all with the same signature:\n\n```\n_multiprocessing.SemLock -> PermissionError: [Errno 1] Operation not permitted\n```\n\nThe sandbox refuses POSIX semaphore creation. This was proven environmental rather than change-induced by running the identical command against an unmodified `origin/main` baseline and observing the same failures. `make lint` and `make typecheck` pass in the same environment, so the breakage is confined to the test-execution half of `verify`.\n\n## Why this matters\n\nThe consequence is not slow tests — it is a **missing gate**. Agents working in this environment currently cannot run the contract at all, so every change is pushed on `lint` + `typecheck` + targeted `pytest` and relies on CI's `Verified` vote to catch the large family of invariants that this repo enforces through pytest rather than through lint scripts: the ratchets' baseline-vs-tree comparisons, the public-API surface census, CI-workflow parity, generated-artifact and docs drift, and the whole-tree AST policy scans. `AGENTS.md` documents an incident on 2026-09-04 in which three changes were pushed red by exactly those tests after their authors saw `lint` and `typecheck` green. That is precisely the failure mode this environment now forces on every agent by default.\n\nA pre-push contract that cannot run in the environment its users work in is not a contract.\n\n## Required outcome\n\n`make verify` must produce a trustworthy verdict in **both** environments:\n\n- **CI** — unchanged. It must keep selecting exactly what the gating `ubuntu-latest, py3.13` cell selects (`not integration and not external`), with no reduction in what is executed and no new ability to pass while tests are skipped.\n- **Local sandboxed workstation** — it must run to completion and report a real pass/fail, rather than aborting on an environment capability it does not actually need.\n\n## Investigation required first\n\nDo not adjust the runner before establishing which component requests the semaphore. Candidates to distinguish:\n\n1. The parallel test runner itself (`make test` defaults to `PYTEST_WORKERS=4`), where parallelism is an execution detail and serial execution would preserve the selection contract exactly.\n2. Individual tests or fixtures that use `multiprocessing` / `ProcessPoolExecutor` directly, where the capability is part of what is under test and silently skipping them WOULD weaken the contract.\n\nThese two have materially different correct fixes, and the 52 failures may be a mix of both. Identify the actual distribution before proposing anything.\n\n## Constraints\n\n- Degrading the local run must never silently reduce coverage. If any test genuinely requires a capability the sandbox denies, that must be reported explicitly in the run's output and summary — a verdict that hides skipped tests is worse than the current hard failure, because it reads as success.\n- CI behaviour must not be weakened to accommodate the sandbox. Any fallback must be conditional on detecting the denied capability, not on a hand-set flag that could drift on in CI.\n- Detection must be a real capability probe, not a hostname, username, or environment-name guess.\n- The mechanism-delta ratchet is shrink-only and fails on any new unadmitted mechanism. If this fix needs a new env var or config key, it must be justified at its definition site with the documented `mechanism-ok` marker, or avoided.\n\n## Escalation\n\nIf a solution meeting all of the above is not achievable while following sound CI and DevOps practice — in particular if the only options are to weaken the CI contract or to let local runs report success while skipping tests — do NOT pick the lesser evil. Stop and escalate to the operator with the tradeoff stated plainly.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] The component(s) requesting POSIX semaphores are identified, with the failure count attributed between runner-level parallelism and test-level use of multiprocessing.\n- [ ] `make verify` runs to completion and produces a pass/fail verdict on a sandboxed local workstation.\n- [ ] CI continues to select and execute exactly what it selects today; no test that runs in CI today is skipped there after this change.\n- [ ] Any locally unrunnable test is reported explicitly in the local run's output and summary rather than silently skipped.\n- [ ] Capability detection is a genuine runtime probe, not an environment-name heuristic.\n- [ ] `docs/local-dev-env.md` documents the local behaviour and how it differs from CI.\n- [ ] The change adds no unadmitted mechanism, or justifies any addition at its definition site.\n","id":"0eba-cfd8-34b0-4cb7","parent_id":"","priority":1,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"make verify cannot run under the agent sandbox: POSIX semaphore denial aborts the pre-push contract"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1789669835710639806,"uuid":"505a0bd8-977a-48a5-9c85-135cf411ea9b"}